(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-anti-infectives-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"ENT Anti-infectives & Antifungals Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is amoxicillin given at a high dose rather than a standard one in acute otitis media?","choices":["It shortens the course to a single day","It overcomes Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance","It penetrates the middle ear only at high concentrations","It covers Pseudomonas, which standard doses miss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pneumococcal resistance here is a matter of degree rather than all-or-nothing, so raising the concentration restores activity. That is the whole argument for the higher dose. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Otic preparations usually combine an anti-infective with a glucocorticoid. What does each component do?","choices":["The anti-infective inhibits bacterial growth and the steroid reduces inflammatory cytokines","The steroid kills the organism and the anti-infective reduces swelling","Both components are antibacterial and act synergistically","The steroid improves penetration of the anti-infective through the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two separate jobs in one bottle, which is also why the combinations cost more than a plain antibiotic drop. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"When is injected ceftriaxone used in acute otitis media?","choices":["As first line in any child under two","Whenever the tympanic membrane has perforated","Only after culture confirms a resistant organism","For failed therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both routes to it are practical rather than microbiological: the oral agents have not worked, or they cannot be kept down. Or when oral medication is not tolerated. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why does ketoconazole interact with so many other medicines?","choices":["It is highly protein bound and displaces other drugs","It induces its own metabolism over time","It inhibits CYP3A4","It is eliminated unchanged by the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It raises the level of anything cleared by that route, which makes the interaction list a property of the enzyme rather than of any one drug pairing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which hepatic adverse effects are described with ketoconazole?","choices":["Hepatitis, cirrhosis and hepatic failure","Gallstones with episodes of biliary colic","Hepatic vein thrombosis and portal hypertension","Fatty liver with no change in liver enzymes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A full spectrum from a blood test abnormality through to organ failure, which is why liver monitoring belongs with this drug. Deranged liver enzymes appear before any of these. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does nystatin work?","choices":["It inhibits cytochrome P450 within the fungal cell","It inhibits protein synthesis in the fungal cell","It binds membrane sterols and increases permeability","It prevents the fungus adhering to the oral mucosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It acts on what is already in the membrane rather than on the machinery that builds it, which is why it works on contact. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which single-dose option is offered for streptococcal pharyngitis in a patient unlikely to finish a course?","choices":["A ten-day course of oral azithromycin","A ten-day course of oral amoxicillin","Benzathine penicillin G by intramuscular injection","Intramuscular ceftriaxone given over three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One injection replaces the whole oral course, which is precisely its point when adherence is in doubt. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why is a rapid streptococcal test used before treating a sore throat?","choices":["To identify which antibiotic the organism is sensitive to","To exclude infectious mononucleosis","To decide the length of the course","To prevent over-prescribing, since most pharyngitis is viral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Only a minority of sore throats are streptococcal, so testing is what keeps the antibiotic away from the rest. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Besides liver and cardiac effects, what else is described with ketoconazole?","choices":["Hyperkalaemia and hypertension","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia together","Peripheral neuropathy","Hyperlipidaemia and orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neither is obvious for an antifungal, and the orthostatic drop is the one a patient will actually notice. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the specific concern with polymyxin B reaching the middle ear?","choices":["Cochlear damage and hearing loss","Facial nerve palsy","Chronic perforation of the drum","Vestibular failure alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The harm is permanent and the drug was given for an ear complaint, which is what makes checking the drum first worth the trouble. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which agents are listed as available otic anti-infectives?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, and two steroid combinations","Amoxicillin, azithromycin and cefdinir, as drops","Gentamicin and tobramycin, given as ear drops","Nystatin and ketoconazole, given as ear drops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two plain fluoroquinolones and two combinations, and the combinations are where the restrictions live. In full: ciprofloxacin, ciprofloxacin with dexamethasone, neomycin with polymyxin B and hydrocortisone, and ofloxacin. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why does bacterial rhinosinusitis start with amoxicillin-clavulanate when otitis media does not?","choices":["The sinuses are harder for antibiotics to penetrate","Beta-lactamase producing Haemophilus influenzae is common in sinusitis","Sinusitis is caused by entirely different organisms","Clavulanate has independent antibacterial activity against these organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The clavulanate exists to inhibit that enzyme, so the frequency of the enzyme is what decides whether it is needed from the outset. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the standard of therapy for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["High-dose amoxicillin alone","Azithromycin","Doxycycline","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike otitis media, this one starts with the clavulanate already on board, because beta-lactamase producing Haemophilus influenzae is common here. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What proportion of acute pharyngitis is due to group A streptococcus?","choices":["The clear majority of cases","Almost all cases in adults","A minority of cases, with most being viral","It is too rare to be worth testing for"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That imbalance is the entire argument for testing before prescribing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which otic ingredient is specifically not recommended when the tympanic membrane is perforated or tubes are in place?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Polymyxin B","Dexamethasone","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With a route to the middle ear it risks cochlear damage and hearing loss, which makes an intact drum the thing to establish before prescribing it. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does ketoconazole work?","choices":["It binds to the sterols already present in the fungal cell membrane","It inhibits fungal DNA synthesis","It alters fungal cell wall permeability by inhibiting cytochrome P450","It blocks fungal cell wall glucan synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The same enzyme family it acts on in the fungus explains its behaviour in the patient, which is where its interactions come from. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A child with acute otitis media has a penicillin allergy. Which agents are offered?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin","Cefdinir or azithromycin","Clindamycin or cefixime","Vancomycin or linezolid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed, though the macrolide is the weaker of the two here because pneumococcal resistance to macrolides runs high. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is changing the organisms that cause acute otitis media?","choices":["Vaccination","Rising antibiotic resistance alone","Increasing use of tympanostomy tubes","Climate and seasonal variation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The aetiology is shifting on the back of vaccination, which is why the proportions attached to each organism are not fixed and why they keep being revised. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A patient with streptococcal pharyngitis is penicillin-allergic. Which agents are offered?","choices":["Cefdinir, cefixime or ceftriaxone","Cephalexin, clindamycin or azithromycin","Levofloxacin or moxifloxacin","Doxycycline or trimethoprim"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three alternatives rather than one, which is worth holding onto because the allergy lists differ between the ear, the sinuses and the throat. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
